      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks! You\u0027re right about the mechanism. Like upstream, `vm_iomap_memory()` maps from the kernel page that contains `start`, so a 4KB process sees the resource at `start \u0026 ~PAGE_MASK_KERNEL`, exactly where a native process sees it. As far as I can see, no GKI caller passes a `start` that isn\u0027t 16KB-aligned: open-dice requires an aligned base, and the PCM iomem, STM and fbdev users aren\u0027t built.\n\nOn the stale `vm_slice_off`: it\u0027s the slice of the process\u0027s own offset, so a driver that uses `vm_pgoff` as a cookie should reject a non-zero `vma_slice_off()`, as `perf_mmap()` does. The effect is bounded, since `vma_kernel_pages()` counts the slice. aosp/4368009 keeps it too (through `vma_file_offset()`), so squashing it wouldn\u0027t change this.\n\naosp/4368009 also needs `remap_pfn_range_slice()` (aosp/4367963) and `vma_aligned_size()` (aosp/4367949), which would pull about 150 lines from later batches into b9. I\u0027d rather keep it as a follow-up in Kelvin\u0027s series.",
